      {
        "id": 93289,
        "name": "Ameena",
        "username": "Ameena",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ameena/{size}/1907_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-02-23T14:09:44.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Bunker's Mode restrictions on doing stuff apply to <span class=\"bbcode-i\">everything</span>, not just the phases - if something is restricting you from doing something only in a certain phase, it will say so - for example, Unity's bots specify that they \"cannot be played during your Play phase\", meaning that if it's <span class=\"bbcode-i\">someone else's</span> Play phase (or any other phase), you're absolutely allowed to put out a bot. The only way Bunker can perform an action which gets around a Mode card is via Ammo Drop, which explicitly says that you can ignore the \"cannot draw cards\" part of your Modes when it goes off. One phrase which came up a while back on the forum somewhere (spoken Christopher himself, I believe), is that \"Cannot trumps can\".</p>\n<p>Note that Bunker can get around his Mode restrictions via something like Omni-10's Timeshift, as this has you <span class=\"bbcode-i\">reveal</span> a card, and then <span class=\"bbcode-i\">put it into play</span> (or into the trash), which isn't the same as <span class=\"bbcode-i\">playing</span> a card.</p>\n<p>For the case of Darkwatch Fixer, you're right about them being separate - if he needed to destroy something in order to hit something, the power would be phrased along the lines of \"Destroy one hero ongoing/equipment card. If you do, deal one target 3 melée damage\". As it is, first you hit something, then you check to see if there are any hero ongoing/equipment cards in play, and if so, you <span class=\"bbcode-i\">must</span> destroy one of them. If there are none, then yay, you got to hit something for free :)/ This power combos well with Bloody Knuckles since you can hit something for +2 damage and then immediately destroy Bloody Knuckles (and avoid getting hit for +2 damage in any subsequent turns).</p>\n<p>Having two separate, unconnected parts of a power can also be seen with examples like the Wraith's Stun Bolts (you can nerf a target's damage even if it has soak preventing you from actually hurting it with the Stun Bolt) and Nightmist's base power, Investigate (you can draw two cards even if unable to hurt yourself for whatever reason).</p>",
        "post_number": 2,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:19:28.492Z",
